WEBVTT

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:11.340
<v Ira Glass>I didn't see this one happen. But Ben, one of the producer here at This American Life , he saw it. He was in the news room here at our home station, WBEZ Chicago, one day, when Dan Blumberg arrived for work.
<v Ira Glass>我没亲眼见过这个。但我们这儿的制作人之一本（Ben）见过。有一天，他在我们电台总部——芝加哥WBEZ——工作时，丹·布鲁姆伯格（Dan Blumberg）来上班了。

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:21.010
<v Ben>And Dan walks in. And he's just swollen, like everywhere. Like, his eyes-- here, I'll do the face. It was like--
<v Ben>丹走进来时，整个人都肿了。就像……到处都肿了。他的眼睛——等等，我给你表演一下那个表情。就像是……

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:24.340
<v Ira Glass>Oh, wow. You're puffing out your cheeks and squinting your eyes.
<v Ira Glass>噢，天哪。你在嘟起脸颊，眯着眼睛。

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:52.490
<v Ben>Yeah, yeah, yeah. I mean, his ears were like cauliflower. I was like, Dan, are you OK? And he says, "It's OK. Last night, I ate some crab." And everybody's reaction is, of course, oh, you're allergic! You need to go to the hospital! And he's like, no, no, no. This happens pretty regularly because I love crab. And I'm allergic, but it only happens one out of every three times.
<v Ben>对，对，对。我是说，他的耳朵像花椰菜一样。我问他：“丹，你没事吧？”他说：“没事，昨晚我吃了一点螃蟹。”大家的反应当然是：“噢，你过敏了！你得去医院！”而他却说：“不，不，不。这经常发生，因为我太爱吃螃蟹了。虽然我过敏，但每吃三次才会有一次反应。”

00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:54.190
<v Ira Glass>What?
<v Ira Glass>什么？

00:00:54.190 --> 00:01:09.990
<v Ben>Yeah. It was a calculated risk. One out of every three times, he would turn into, like, the Stay Puft Marshmallow Man. Yeah, so, I mean I suppose this is the part where you'd bring Dan in.
<v Ben>是啊。这是一种经过计算的风险。每吃三次螃蟹，他就得变成像“棉花糖人”那样。所以，我想这该是你请丹上场的时候了。

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:11.620
<v Dan>I should have called in sick that day.
<v Dan>那天我本该请病假的。

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:13.110
<v Ira Glass>This, of course, is Dan.
<v Ira Glass>当然，这就是丹。

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:22.420
<v Dan>I think that morning it was mostly the eyes that would have been kind of freaking people out, very heavy eyelids, kind of probably a sunken look to them.
<v Dan>我想那天早上，主要是他的眼睛会把人吓坏，眼皮非常沉重，看起来可能有点凹陷。

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:40.670
<v Ira Glass>Dan did confirm everything that Ben said and more. He's been allergic to crab and lobster since he was a kid. The night before this incident, he had eaten crab. And he got the worst reaction he'd had in years. It was a wake up call. Not that he going to stop eating crab and lobster-- no, no. He was going to keep doing that. But now, he'd do it--
<v Ira Glass>丹证实了本所说的一切，甚至更多。他从小就对螃蟹和龙虾过敏。事件发生的前一天晚上，他吃了螃蟹，结果出现了多年来最严重的一次反应。这给他敲响了警钟。但这并不意味着他会停止吃螃蟹和龙虾——不，不。他还是会继续吃。但现在，他会……

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:50.690
<v Dan>Only if I have Benadryl as a sort of aperitif. And only if I have an inhaler just in case it were to spread to my lungs. And I have an EpiPen.
<v Dan>只有在我把苯海拉明（Benadryl）当作餐前酒的情况下才会吃。而且必须随身带着吸入器，以防万一过敏反应扩散到肺部。我还准备了一支肾上腺素笔（EpiPen）。

00:01:50.690 --> 00:02:12.540
<v Ira Glass>An EpiPen, if you've never heard of it, is the injection that you stab yourself with in an emergency if you get a life threatening allergic reaction. And this is his system. And he says it's working for him. And no, his doctor does not know. He risks this twice a year or so. He says his wife's not so crazy about it, partly because the Benadryl makes him really sleepy.
<v Ira Glass>如果你从没听说过肾上腺素笔，它是一种注射器，如果你出现危及生命的过敏反应，就在紧急情况下扎自己一下。这就是他的“系统”。他说这对他很有效。而且，不，他的医生并不知道。他每年大约会冒两次这样的险。他说他妻子不太喜欢这样，部分原因是苯海拉明会让他非常困倦。

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:23.000
<v Dan>But the poisoning myself, it's not that bad. Like I said, I get sleepy from the Benadryl. That's the worst part, I get really tired.
<v Dan>但毒害自己其实也没那么糟糕。就像我说的，苯海拉明会让我犯困。那是唯一糟糕的部分，我感到非常疲惫。

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:27.910
<v Ira Glass>But if you find yourself saying the sentence, the poisoning myself is not that bad--
<v Ira Glass>但如果你发现自己说出“毒害自己其实也没那么糟糕”这样的话……

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:38.670
<v Dan>Yeah, I mean, I think there's something probably to that. You know, I like it. What can I say?
<v Dan>是的，我想这可能确实说明了什么。你知道，我就是喜欢它。我能说什么呢？

00:02:38.670 --> 00:03:02.410
<v Ira Glass>This whole thing made me think of some book that you would read about the decline of the Roman Empire, people eating so much that they would have to throw up so they could eat some more. This is like someday, people are going to write, what was America like back in its heyday? Well, what it was like was people with severe allergies would eat the thing that they are allergic to but just have meds at the ready to stab themselves with and cure themselves.
<v Ira Glass>整件事让我想起了一些关于罗马帝国衰落的书，人们吃得太多，不得不催吐以便能吃得更多。这就像有朝一日，人们会写道：美国的全盛时期是什么样的？嗯，那时候的情况就是：严重过敏的人会吃掉他们过敏的东西，但随时准备好药物扎自己一下来治疗。

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:10.020
<v Ira Glass>But, you know, reaching out to friends, and family, and to strangers over the internet this past two weeks, I have learned that what Dan did is actually not that uncommon.
<v Ira Glass>不过，过去两周我联系了朋友、家人和互联网上的陌生人，我了解到丹的做法其实并不罕见。

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:18.020
<v Ruthie>I do have willpower. But when it comes to the whole pizza thing, I don't hesitate. I just go ahead and eat it.
<v Ruthie>我有自制力，但一提到披萨，我就毫不犹豫。我直接就吃了。

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:29.670
<v Ira Glass>Ruthie Zinchuck in Charlotte, North Carolina, is lactose intolerant. She can't digest milk, or cheese, or any dairy product, which is a problem because as a transplanted Jersey girl, she says, she likes her pizza.
<v Ira Glass>北卡罗来纳州夏洛特的露西·金查克（Ruthie Zinchuck）患有乳糖不耐症。她无法消化牛奶、奶酪或任何乳制品，这很麻烦，因为作为一个从新泽西搬来的女孩，她说，她喜欢吃披萨。

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:41.580
<v Ruthie>Well, we do have Pizza Hut. Out of nowhere, I'll get severe stomach cramping. And I'll have to go to the bathroom like immediately. It's no joke.
<v Ruthie>好吧，我们有必胜客。冷不丁地，我就会出现严重的胃痉挛。我得立刻去洗手间。这不是开玩笑的。

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:43.490
<v Ira Glass>And so how often do you have pizza now?
<v Ira Glass>那你现在多久吃一次披萨？

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:45.800
<v Ruthie>I would say at least a couple times a week.
<v Ruthie>我想至少一周几次。

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:47.360
<v Ira Glass>Oh, a couple times a week?
<v Ira Glass>噢，一周几次？

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:59.050
<v Ruthie>Yeah. It's always our go-to when we don't know what to eat. My fiancee and I are like, well, I guess pizza will work. And he'll always just look at me and say, "Really? Are you sure?"
<v Ruthie>对。当我们不知道吃什么的时候，这总是我们的首选。我和未婚夫会说，好吧，我想披萨就行。他总是看着我说：“真的吗？你确定？”

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:18.150
<v Ira Glass>In Cleveland, Ohio, Elise Hagisfeld, and her older sister, and her younger sister, and her mom, and her daughter are all allergic to tomatoes, citrus, pineapple, strawberries in various combinations. Elise herself gets hives. It makes her face blotchy. She gets itchy. But they all continue to eat this stuff.
<v Ira Glass>在俄亥俄州克利夫兰，伊丽丝·哈吉斯菲尔德（Elise Hagisfeld），还有她的姐姐、妹妹、妈妈和女儿，都对西红柿、柑橘、菠萝、草莓的不同组合过敏。伊丽丝本人会起荨麻疹，脸上起红斑，浑身发痒。但她们都继续吃这些东西。

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:32.690
<v Elise>I don't know if I have any great stories about it because we just do it all the time. We apparently are a family that has absolutely no self-control or good sense. I'm not sure which, but whatever. I like it!
<v Elise>我不知道关于这个我有什么精彩的故事，因为我们总是这样。显然我们这个家庭完全没有自制力，或者说缺乏常识。我不知道是哪种，但无所谓。我喜欢！

00:04:32.690 --> 00:05:04.470
<v Ira Glass>Which is, of course, pretty much what Dan said, and what my senior producer's mother-in-law told me, too. Her name is Barbara Melman, and I reached her on vacation. She said that a couple years ago, after some abdominal surgery, she had a few episodes where she got incredibly sick, throwing up, rushing to the emergency room, getting an IV, an anti-nausea medication, until she realized that each time this happened, she'd eaten popcorn, or nuts, or trail mix before. And so she cut those from her diet. But over time, she has snuck that stuff back in.
<v Ira Glass>这当然和丹说的差不多，也是我高级制作人的岳母告诉我的。她叫芭芭拉·梅尔曼（Barbara Melman），我是在她度假时联系到她的。她说几年前，做过腹部手术后，她有几次病得非常严重，呕吐，被送进急诊室，输液，服用止吐药，直到她意识到每次发生这种情况前，她都吃过爆米花、坚果或什锦杂果。于是她从饮食中去掉了这些东西，但随着时间推移，她又偷偷地把它们加了回来。

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:10.010
<v Barbara>And being on vacation now, I've got a room full of popcorn and trail mix.
<v Barbara>现在度假的时候，我的房间里全都是爆米花和什锦杂果。

00:05:10.010 --> 00:05:11.460
<v Ira Glass>You do? Right now?
<v Ira Glass>你有吗？现在？

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:13.990
<v Barbara>I do. I'm staring at it as we're speaking, mm-hm.
<v Barbara>我有。我们说话的时候我正盯着它们看，嗯。

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:17.140
<v Ira Glass>Wait, don't you think you're playing with fire?
<v Ira Glass>等等，你不觉得你在玩火吗？

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:31.430
<v Barbara>Well, you know, it's not going to kill me. And if it does, I guess I won't know. It won't kill me. No, I mean, it's throwing up. It's not like I have to go through surgery.
<v Barbara>嗯，你知道，它不会杀了我。如果真的杀了，我想我也不会知道。它杀不了我。不，我是说，这只是呕吐。又不像是得做手术。

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:34.860
<v Ira Glass>Just a quick trip to the hospital, an IV, some medication.
<v Ira Glass>只是去趟急诊室，输个液，吃点药。

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:40.300
<v Barbara>A few hours, mm-hm. And meet some nice people and leave.
<v Barbara>几个小时而已，嗯。还能认识些不错的人，然后离开。

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:45.750
<v Michelle>The ER is a virtual laboratory of dysfunctional behaviors and bad choices.
<v Michelle>急诊室就是一个功能障碍行为和错误选择的虚拟实验室。

00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:48.630
<v Ira Glass>Do you just feel like, as a doctor, what is wrong with people?
<v Ira Glass>作为医生，你是否会觉得人们到底是怎么了？

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:51.680
<v Michelle>Oh, every day, every day.
<v Michelle>噢，每天，每天都是这样。

00:05:51.680 --> 00:06:03.250
<v Ira Glass>This is Michelle DeVito, an emergency room doctor at an urban hospital in Washington, D.C. And she says that nearly every day, she sees somebody with a food allergy who has eaten the food that they're not supposed and ended up in her ER.
<v Ira Glass>这是米歇尔·德维托（Michelle DeVito），华盛顿特区一家城市医院的急诊医生。她说几乎每天都能看到有人因为食物过敏而吃了不该吃的东西，最后被送进她的急诊室。

00:06:03.250 --> 00:06:07.110
<v Michelle>It's such a thing that it's not up for debate.
<v Michelle>这已经是一种普遍现象，没什么好辩论的。

00:06:07.110 --> 00:06:25.110
<v Ira Glass>Dr. DeVito told me story after story there are the people with esophageal problems who are not supposed to eat steak who come in again and again. The same people with steak caught in their throats, looking guilty, avoiding eye contact with her, requiring a gastroenterologist to be called in for an emergency endoscopy.
<v Ira Glass>德维托医生给我讲了一个又一个故事：有些食道有问题的人不该吃牛排，却一次又一次地来。同样的人，牛排卡在喉咙里，一脸愧疚，不敢和她对视，还得叫胃肠科医生来做紧急内窥镜检查。

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:32.610
<v Ira Glass>How much of what you see in an ER is people who are their own worst enemies?
<v Ira Glass>你在急诊室看到的情况中，有多少是属于“自讨苦吃”的？

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:55.210
<v Michelle>Oh, I think maybe half. I think that the smokers, the out-of-control diabetics, the people who get in a car accident from text messaging on the phone-- I think so much of the pathology we see in the ER is a result of bad choices. And some of them, decades of bad choices.
<v Michelle>噢，我想大概一半吧。我想吸烟者、失控的糖尿病患者、因为发短信出车祸的人……我认为我们在急诊室看到的很多病理结果都是错误选择的结果。其中一些甚至是几十年的错误选择。

00:06:55.210 --> 00:07:45.820
<v Ira Glass>I think there once we've become adults, most of us are aware of the different ways in which we can be our own worst enemies, not just with what we eat and with our health, but in how we deal with people, how we handle certain situations. Well, in this hour, prepare to meet people who make my problems and yours when it comes to this seem like nothing. We have people who create problems for themselves in spectacular, life-changing ways that they do not know how to stop. We have one man who finds himself suddenly unable to do his job, others who are sabotaging relationships, and they cannot themselves when they do it. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>我认为一旦我们成为了成年人，我们大多数人都意识到了自己成为自身最大敌人的不同方式，不仅是在饮食和健康方面，还有在处理人际关系和特定情况的方式上。在这个小时里，准备好见见那些让你的问题显得微不足道的人吧。有些人以惊人的、改变生活的方式给自己制造麻烦，却不知道如何停止。有一个人突然无法胜任自己的工作，其他人则在破坏关系，当他们这样做时，自己也控制不了。来自芝加哥WBEZ，这是《美国生活》（This American Life），由国际公共广播电台（PRI）发行。请继续收听。

00:07:45.820 --> 00:08:02.940
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Aces are Wild. Ken Fueson is a baseball fan and a lifelong supporter of the St. Louis Cardinals. And he had the same experience that a lot of Cardinal fans had back on October 3, 2000, watching his team play the Atlanta Braves. It was the first game of the National League playoffs.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，王牌疯狂（Aces are Wild）。肯·福森（Ken Fueson）是一位棒球迷，也是圣路易斯红雀队的终身支持者。2000年10月3日那天，他和许多红雀队球迷有了同样的经历，当时他在看红雀队对阵亚特兰大勇士队。那是国家联盟季后赛的第一场比赛。

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:15.970
<v Ken>I remember exactly where I was. I was at Johnny's Hall of Fame, which is a sports bar in Des Moines. I was sitting at a table. I had a big glass of iced tea and a patty melt sandwich. The Cardinals scored six runs in the first inning. This is going to be fantastic.
<v Ken>我记得我当时确切的位置。我在得梅因的一家运动酒吧“约翰尼名人堂”（Johnny's Hall of Fame）。我坐在一张桌子旁。手里有一大杯冰茶和一份肉饼三明治。红雀队在第一局就得了六分。我想，这太棒了。

00:08:15.970 --> 00:08:26.070
<v Ira Glass>The starting pitcher for the Cardinals in this game was Rick Ankiel, a young phenom who'd had a great rookie season. He pitched two innings, no problem. But in the third, he lost control.
<v Ira Glass>红雀队在这场比赛中的先发投手是里克·安基尔（Rick Ankiel），一位在菜鸟赛季表现出色的年轻天才。他投了两局，没问题。但在第三局，他失控了。

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:55.820
<v Ken>I don't know where the first wild pitch went, but it was like-- you thought to yourself, you know, it's slipped out of his hands. It's like one of those scenes that they show, like the pitcher stumbling and the ball goes up in the air. And then it happened again. You're still kind of thinking, OK, maybe the nervousness of the moment, or whatever. But you're not thinking, this is going to be a big problem. He's going to settle down. And then, it keeps happening.
<v Ken>我不知道第一个狂投（wild pitch）飞向了哪里，但就像是……你当时想，他一定是手滑了。就像电影里那种投手踉跄、球飞向空中的场景。然后它又发生了。你还是觉得，好吧，也许是那一刻的紧张，或者其他什么。但你没想过这是一个大问题，你会觉得他会冷静下来的。然后，它就一直发生。

00:08:55.820 --> 00:09:13.880
<v Ken>I'm thinking, there has to be some sort of physical explanation for this. You know, there's something wrong with his arm. This isn't even little league quality wild pitches. It's beyond that. I mean, it's like you would have to try to throw it this badly. And I'm at this bar, and people are laughing at him.
<v Ken>我在想，一定有什么生理上的解释。你知道，他的手臂一定出问题了。这甚至不像是小联盟水平的狂投。这更严重。我是说，就像你必须努力才能投得这么烂一样。我待在酒吧里，人们都在嘲笑他。

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:20.130
<v Ira Glass>Ankiel gets pulled from the game. Cardinals win. And in the next round of playoffs, they're up against the New York Mets.
<v Ira Glass>安基尔被换下场。红雀队赢了。在下一轮季后赛中，他们对阵纽约大都会队。

00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:34.360
<v Ken>And so they bring Ankiel out to try again for the second game of the series. He lasts 2/3rds of an inning. He doesn't even get out the first inning-- throws two wild pitches, walks three. It's literally like watching your kid in a peewee game.
<v Ken>于是他们让安基尔在系列赛的第二场比赛中再次尝试。他只撑了2/3局。他甚至没能投完第一局——投了两个狂投，保送了三次。这简直就像在看你孩子打少儿比赛一样。

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:44.220
<v Commentator>Wild pitch. He's got guys in the third row ducking. Friendlies ducking back there. Bob, you all right down there? You ducking? You feel like you're safe behind that screen?
<v Commentator>狂投。第三排的人都在躲闪。后排的朋友也在躲。鲍勃，你没事吧？你在躲吗？你觉得在那块挡网后面安全吗？

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:54.550
<v Ken>And as a fan, you're just, this can't be happening. It's disbelief. I don't believe this. Then, you get angry at him. What's he trying to do out there? What's going on? And then, you start feeling sorry for him.
<v Ken>作为一个球迷，你只是觉得，这不可能发生。是难以置信。我不相信。然后，你会对他感到生气。他在外面试图干什么？发生了什么？然后，你开始同情他。

00:09:54.550 --> 00:09:56.560
<v Commentator>And this is borderline ridiculous.
<v Commentator>这简直荒谬透顶。

00:09:56.560 --> 00:10:07.980
<v Ken>On a human level-- that's the other horrible part of this-- he's naked in front of the world. He's out there trying to get this back. And it ain't coming back. You feel horrible for him, yeah.
<v Ken>从人的层面来看——这是最可怕的部分——他在全世界面前赤身裸体。他在外面努力想找回状态。但状态就是回不来了。你真的为他感到难过，是的。

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:46.250
<v Ira Glass>Ankiel's bad streak eventually got him sent down to the minor leagues. He stopped pitching, came back to the major leagues as an outfielder. But watching Ankiel that day in the bar made Ken think of a different player from years before, Steve Blass. He played for Pittsburgh. His name has become shorthand for any pitcher who suddenly becomes his own worst enemy and loses the ability to throw the ball. They call it Steve Blass Disease. Blass was an all-star, on the cover of Sports Illustrated . Older fans remember him winning game seven of the 1971 World Series for the Pirates. After the Series, he had a great season in '72, won 19 games.
<v Ira Glass>安基尔糟糕的表现最终导致他被下放到小联盟。他停止了投球，后来作为外野手回到了大联盟。但那天在酒吧里看着安基尔，让肯想起了几年前的另一位球员，史蒂夫·布拉斯（Steve Blass）。他曾为匹兹堡海盗队效力。他的名字已经成为所有突然成为自身最大敌人、丧失投球能力的投手的代名词。他们称之为“史蒂夫·布拉斯症候群”。布拉斯曾经入选全明星赛，还上过《体育画报》封面。老球迷记得他在1971年世界大赛第七场为海盗队获胜。系列赛结束后，他在72年表现出色，赢了19场比赛。

00:10:46.250 --> 00:11:19.940
<v Ira Glass>And then, in 1973, for no apparent reason, no physical injury of any kind, he stopped being able to throw strikes. He never recovered, and ended his career. He was a player falling apart for purely psychological reasons, which can happen to any athlete in any sport, of course. But the suddenness of Blass' fall and the visibility of his fall-- he was one of the leading pitchers in baseball, and he just lost it-- it got people's attention. And if you want to understand what Steve Blass Disease is about, one place you can go is Steve Blass.
<v Ira Glass>然后，在1973年，没有明显的原因，没有任何身体伤害，他突然无法投出好球了。他从未恢复，结束了自己的职业生涯。他是一位纯粹因为心理原因而崩溃的球员，当然，这在任何运动中的任何运动员身上都可能发生。但布拉斯崩溃的突然性和可见性——他是棒球界顶尖投手之一，却突然失控了——这引起了人们的注意。如果你想了解什么是“史蒂夫·布拉斯症候群”，你可以去问史蒂夫·布拉斯本人。

00:11:19.940 --> 00:11:30.690
<v Steve Blass>So I'm sitting in my living room with my wife. We're watching the playoffs with the Cardinals. And Rick Ankiel starts throwing the ball all over the place-- behind people, to the backstop.
<v Steve Blass>所以我正和妻子坐在客厅里。我们正在看红雀队的季后赛。里克·安基尔开始把球扔得到处都是——扔向人群后面，扔向护网。

00:11:30.690 --> 00:11:34.900
<v Ira Glass>Turns out Steve Blass was watching that same Cardinals game that Ken Fueson saw.
<v Ira Glass>事实证明，史蒂夫·布拉斯也在看肯·福森看到的同一场红雀队的比赛。

00:11:34.900 --> 00:11:59.110
<v Steve Blass>And I turned to my wife, Karen, and I said, "Karen, I'm predicting within the next five minutes, the phone will ring." Well, three minutes later, it rang. And I picked it up and said, "Hi, Paul." My good friend, Paul Meyer, beat writer for the Pirates who was covering the playoffs for the Pittsburgh Post-Gazette . He said, "How do you know it's me?" I said, "Well, of course it's you. Somebody just went off the planet again."
<v Steve Blass>我对妻子卡伦（Karen）说：“卡伦，我敢预测接下来的五分钟内，电话会响。”嗯，三分钟后，电话响了。我拿起电话说：“嘿，保罗。”我的好朋友保罗·迈耶（Paul Meyer），海盗队的随队记者，正在为《匹兹堡邮报》报道季后赛。他说：“你怎么知道是我？”我说：“嗯，当然是你。又有人从地球上掉下去了。”

00:11:59.110 --> 00:12:02.140
<v Ira Glass>How many interviews have you given about this over the years, do you think?
<v Ira Glass>这些年来你大概接受过多少次关于这个话题的采访？

00:12:02.140 --> 00:12:04.060
<v Steve Blass>I have an 800 number.
<v Steve Blass>我有专门的800号码。

00:12:04.060 --> 00:12:05.300
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHS]
<v Ira Glass>[笑声]

00:12:05.300 --> 00:12:31.540
<v Steve Blass>I mean, whenever anybody-- when Rick Ankiel imploded in the playoffs, when Mark Wohlers was doing his thing, Steve Sax, Chuck Knoblauch And I know why there's interest. I understand. I understand that. People are making a living. It's a curious subject. It's a fascinating subject to go from 19 wins, to three, to none, and then out. So I've had a great deal of patience with it.
<v Steve Blass>我是说，每当有谁……当里克·安基尔在季后赛崩溃时，当马克·沃尔斯（Mark Wohlers）遇到麻烦时，史蒂夫·萨克斯（Steve Sax），查克·诺布劳奇（Chuck Knoblauch）……我知道为什么人们感兴趣。我理解。我明白。人们也要以此谋生。这是一个好奇的话题。从19胜，到3胜，到无胜，然后出局，这是一个引人入胜的话题。所以我对此一直很有耐心。

00:12:31.540 --> 00:12:51.360
<v Ira Glass>Steve Blass turns 70 this coming Wednesday. And next month, he's publishing an autobiography called A Pirate for Life . The title refers to his 10 years as a player, and to the nearly three decades after that that he served as a Pirates color commentator on the air. He says that he wrote the book to tell the story that you can come back from the kind of public collapse that he suffered.
<v Ira Glass>史蒂夫·布拉斯下周三就要70岁了。下个月，他将出版一本自传，书名叫《海盗一生》（A Pirate for Life）。书名指的是他作为球员的10年，以及此后近三十年作为海盗队评论员的时光。他说他写这本书是为了告诉大家，你可以从他遭受的那种公开崩溃中走出来。

00:12:51.360 --> 00:13:13.200
<v Ira Glass>In the immediate aftermath, he got a job selling school rings. He said he drank too much. He says it was hard to go out to the grocery store and be recognized by people. The book covers, but does not dwell on, how his pitching career came apart, which he calls "the abyss." He says he can't name exactly when his problem began, what game it was. He remembers it as just a gradual slide.
<v Ira Glass>在接下来的日子里，他找到了一份卖校友戒指的工作。他说他喝得太多了。他说很难出门去杂货店，因为会被人认出来。书里提到了他的投球生涯是如何崩溃的——他称之为“深渊”，但并没有过度纠结。他说他无法说出问题是从什么时候开始的，是哪场比赛。他记得那只是一个渐进的下滑。

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:35.630
<v Steve Blass>There was not one specific issue, item, time, at bat, game. And to this day, I don't know why. I don't know what caused that. There's still frustration in me because I never had a sore arm, and I had the perfect body. You know, I wasn't heavy or anything. But I still don't know exactly what caused it.
<v Steve Blass>没有一个具体的问题、事项、时间点、击球或比赛。直到今天，我仍然不知道为什么。我不知道是什么导致了这一切。我心里依然有挫败感，因为我从来没有受过伤，而且我有完美的体格。你知道，我并不胖什么的。但我仍然不知道到底是什么导致的。

00:13:35.630 --> 00:13:40.220
<v Ira Glass>I've been going back and reading. And I saw Roger Angell wrote a piece years ago for The New Yorker .
<v Ira Glass>我回去读了一些东西。我看到罗杰·安吉尔（Roger Angell）多年前为《纽约客》写过一篇文章。

00:13:40.220 --> 00:13:40.820
<v Steve Blass>Wonderful piece.
<v Steve Blass>非常棒的文章。

00:13:40.820 --> 00:14:02.270
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, a really beautiful story. And he tries to figure out, well, what game was it? And he names an April, 1973, game with the Cubs that you pitched. And then he says, maybe it's that game. And then, there's another game on June 11 with the Braves in Atlanta. And I was wondering, do you even remember either of those games? Like, do those games stand out as significant?
<v Ira Glass>是的，一个非常优美的故事。他试图搞清楚，嗯，那到底是哪场比赛？他指出了你投的一场1973年4月对阵小熊队的比赛。然后他说，也许是那场。然后，6月11日还有一场对阵亚特兰大勇士队的比赛。我想知道，你甚至还记得那两场比赛吗？就像，那两场比赛作为关键点脱颖而出吗？

00:14:02.270 --> 00:14:32.630
<v Steve Blass>Um, I don't remember the Cubs game. if he's referring to the game where I came in relief in Atlanta, which I think was the case, I remember it more than any other game I've pitched in my life because it was just-- it was the absolute bottom of the pit. And I remember after that game, we flew to Cincinnati. And I didn't even go to my hotel room. I just walked the streets of Cincinnati until dawn. And it was just one of the awfulest games and awfulest nights that I've ever had in my life.
<v Steve Blass>嗯，我不记得小熊队那场比赛。如果他指的是我在亚特兰大那场担任中继投手的比赛，我想应该是那场，比起我一生中投过的任何比赛，我记得那场更多，因为那简直是……那是深渊的最底部。我记得那场比赛后，我们飞往辛辛那提。我甚至没回酒店房间。我就在辛辛那提的街头一直走到天亮。那是我一生中最糟糕的比赛，最糟糕的夜晚之一。

00:14:32.630 --> 00:14:43.220
<v Ira Glass>And so what do you remember of the experience of being on the mound during that game? Like, did it occur to you when you were on the mound, OK, something different is happening now that I haven't experienced before?
<v Ira Glass>那你对那场比赛在投手丘上的经历有什么记忆？就像，当你在投手丘上时，你是否意识到，好吧，现在发生了某种我以前从未经历过的不同寻常的事情？

00:14:43.220 --> 00:15:05.950
<v Steve Blass>Yes. That game in Atlanta, I said, there is something tragically wrong here. And I am lost out here. I don't have a clue of what I'm doing. I start to wind up, and I just kind of almost freeze. And there's no flow, there's no rhythm. I knew I shouldn't be out there. But I didn't want to quit. I wanted to keep trying to find it.
<v Steve Blass>是的。在那场亚特兰大的比赛中，我说，这里发生了极其严重的问题。我在这里迷失了。我根本不知道我在做什么。我开始投球姿势，然后我就几乎僵住了。没有流畅感，没有节奏感。我知道我不该在那里。但我不想放弃。我想继续寻找它。

00:15:05.950 --> 00:15:11.670
<v Ira Glass>Roger Angell's account of that game-- I mean, I'm not even sure if I should read this or not.
<v Ira Glass>罗杰·安吉尔对那场比赛的描述……我是说，我甚至不确定我该不该读这个。

00:15:11.670 --> 00:15:13.660
<v Steve Blass>Oh, I know it by heart.
<v Steve Blass>噢，我倒背如流。

00:15:13.660 --> 00:16:01.540
<v Ira Glass>Yeah-- oh, really? All right. Just for listeners, then, so you get called in in the fifth inning. And the Pirates are trailing already. So you're coming into a losing game. They're trailing by 8 to 3, and Roger Angell writes, "Blass walked the first two men he faced. Gave up a stolen base, and a wild pitch, and run-scoring single before retiring the side. In the sixth, Blass walked Darrell Evans. He walked Mike Lum, throwing one pitch behind him in the process, which allowed Evans to move down to second. Dusty Baker singled, driving in a run. Ralph Garr grounded out. Davey Johnson singled, scoring another run. Marty Perez walked. Pitcher Ron Reed singled, driving in two more runs, and was wild pitched to second. Johnny Oates walked. Frank Tepedino singled, driving in two runs. And Steve Blass was finally relieved."
<v Ira Glass>是吗……噢，真的吗？好吧。那为了听众，当时你在第五局被叫上场。海盗队已经落后了。所以你进的是一场正在输的比赛。他们以3比8落后，罗杰·安吉尔写道：“布拉斯保送了面对的前两名打者。丢掉了一个盗垒，一个狂投，还有一个得分安打，才结束了那一局。在第六局，布拉斯保送了达雷尔·埃文斯（Darrell Evans）。他保送了迈克·卢姆（Mike Lum），在这个过程中甚至投了一个球在他身后，这让埃文斯推进到了二垒。达斯蒂·贝克（Dusty Baker）打出一支安打，得了一分。拉尔夫·加尔（Ralph Garr）打出地滚球出局。戴维·约翰逊（Davey Johnson）打出安打，再得一分。马蒂·佩雷斯（Marty Perez）获得保送。投手罗恩·里德（Ron Reed）打出安打，再得两分，并因狂投推进到二垒。约翰尼·奥茨（Johnny Oates）获得保送。弗兰克·特佩迪诺（Frank Tepedino）打出安打，再得两分。史蒂夫·布拉斯终于被换下场。”

00:16:01.540 --> 00:16:31.530
<v Steve Blass>Yeah, yeah. It was awful. It was just-- it was a nightmare. But I've been taught, and developed, and I firmly believe you don't quit. I just wasn't wired that way. And I wanted to be convinced. If it wasn't there, I wanted to be totally convinced. And that's why I pitched all of '73, obviously. And then, all of '74, which was another nightmare in the minor leagues. And then in '75 I said, that's enough. Uncle. I've had enough.
<v Steve Blass>是啊，是啊。太可怕了。那简直是一场噩梦。但我受到的教育、成长环境让我坚信你不能放弃。我的性格就是这样。而且我想让自己死心。如果状态不在了，我想彻底死心。这就是为什么我显然投完了73年整季，然后是74年，那在小联盟又是另一场噩梦。然后在75年我说，够了。我认输。我已经受够了。

00:16:31.530 --> 00:16:48.940
<v Ira Glass>I read you said to a Pittsburgh sports writer named Joe Starkey years ago, "Underneath, very privately and very personally, is a feeling I got cheated." And did you feel that it's almost like some foreign thing had taken over? Like you were hit by something that you couldn't control?
<v Ira Glass>我读到多年前你对匹兹堡体育记者乔·斯塔基（Joe Starkey）说的话：“在内心深处，非常私密地，有一种被欺骗的感觉。”你是否觉得就像某种外来的东西占据了你？就像你被某种无法控制的东西击中了？

00:16:48.940 --> 00:17:19.320
<v Steve Blass>Well, I certainly had no control over it, nor did I understand it. I was just confused. I mean, I would sit up in my backyard, 3 and 4 o'clock in the morning, thinking, my god, what has happened to me? What is this? Did somebody put a curse on me or something? And I never believed that. You know, I don't believe in that junk. But it was just-- it was devastating in that I didn't know what to do about it.
<v Steve Blass>嗯，我确实无法控制它，也不理解它。我只是感到困惑。我是说，我会在凌晨三四点坐在后院，心想：天哪，我到底怎么了？这是什么？是不是有人给我下了咒？我从不相信那种事。你知道，我不相信那些无稽之谈。但这确实令人崩溃，因为我不知道该怎么办。

00:17:19.320 --> 00:17:41.670
<v Ira Glass>In the decades since Steve Blass stopped pitching, researchers have been trying to figure out what to do about it and what is going on when a player falls apart like he did. And they believe that basically the problem comes down to thinking. When an elite athlete is at his or her best, when they're in the zone, their movements are automatic. They're not thinking about how their wrist turns, or their knee bends, or any of the other details.
<v Ira Glass>在史蒂夫·布拉斯停止投球后的几十年里，研究人员一直在试图弄清楚该怎么做，以及当球员像他那样崩溃时到底发生了什么。他们相信问题基本上归结于“思考”。当一名精英运动员处于巅峰状态，当他们处于“状态”中时，他们的动作是自动的。他们并没有在思考手腕如何转动、膝盖如何弯曲或任何其他细节。

00:17:41.670 --> 00:18:05.390
<v Ira Glass>And when researchers bring athletes into the lab with a simulated batting cage, or a putting green, when they tell them to think about the mechanics of what they're doing, to notice where exactly the bat is moving when they're swinging, or how their elbow shifts when they're putting, the athletes-- the overwhelming majority of them-- start to choke. Thinking is the problem.
<v Ira Glass>当研究人员把运动员带到模拟打击笼或推杆果岭的实验室时，当他们告诉运动员去思考动作的力学，去注意挥棒时棒子到底在向哪里移动，或者推杆时手肘如何移动时，运动员——绝大多数——开始“窒息”了。思考才是问题所在。

00:18:05.390 --> 00:18:31.320
<v Ira Glass>And interestingly, two researchers named Steven Weiss and Arthur Reber have a paper called "Curing the Dreaded Steve Blass Disease," which is going to be published in the Journal of Sports Psychology in Action . And they argue that when well-meaning coaches tell an athlete in a slump to focus more on the mechanics-- which they say is common still-- the coach can actually make things worse, not better, because he's telling a player who's already thinking too much to think even more.
<v Ira Glass>有趣的是，两位研究人员斯蒂芬·韦斯（Steven Weiss）和亚瑟·雷伯（Arthur Reber）写了一篇名为《治愈可怕的史蒂夫·布拉斯症候群》的论文，即将在《运动心理学实践》杂志上发表。他们认为，当出于好意的教练告诉陷入低谷的运动员要多关注动作力学——他们说这在现在仍然很常见——教练实际上可能会让情况变得更糟，而不是更好，因为他是在让一个已经思考太多的球员思考得更多。

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:41.380
<v Rick>We have to somehow figure out a way to get you to stop thinking. You have to find some sort of mental distraction that allows them to go back to just rely upon their instincts.
<v Rick>我们必须想办法让你停止思考。你必须找到某种心理干扰，让他们能够回到纯粹依赖本能的状态。

00:18:41.380 --> 00:18:55.010
<v Ira Glass>This is Rick Wolff. He was a baseball player himself in the minor leagues, and he went on to counsel professional athletes whose minds were working against them. Wolff's job was to redirect them, stop them from thinking about the mechanics of what they were doing.
<v Ira Glass>这是里克·沃尔夫（Rick Wolff）。他自己也曾在小联盟做过棒球运动员，后来开始辅导那些大脑与自己作对的职业运动员。沃尔夫的工作是重定向他们，让他们停止思考自己正在做的动作力学。

00:18:55.010 --> 00:19:14.970
<v Rick>So if you're having problems as a pitcher throwing strikes, I need for you to stop thinking about or worrying about that. And as you wind up, I want to think about something totally different. It might be I want you to really focus on the catcher's left shin guard. I'm just trying to get them to stop thinking about where their arm angle is, and how they're delivering the ball to the plate.
<v Rick>所以如果你作为一个投手在投好球时遇到问题，我需要你停止去想或担心那个问题。在你做投球姿势时，我希望你思考一些完全不同的东西。可能我希望你真正专注于捕手的左护胫。我只是想让他们停止思考手臂的角度，以及他们是如何把球投向本垒板的。

00:19:14.970 --> 00:19:16.080
<v Ira Glass>And does that work?
<v Ira Glass>那有效吗？

00:19:16.080 --> 00:19:18.830
<v Rick>Sometimes it does. Sometimes it doesn't.
<v Rick>有时有效，有时无效。

00:19:18.830 --> 00:19:25.510
<v Ira Glass>It just seems like a hard thing because the more you try not to think about something, the more you're thinking about it.
<v Ira Glass>这似乎很难，因为你越试图不去想某件事，你反而想得越多。

00:19:25.510 --> 00:19:29.340
<v Rick>Yeah. I didn't say it was easy.
<v Rick>是的。我没说这很简单。

00:19:29.340 --> 00:19:35.100
<v Ira Glass>Do most of them get over it? Do most of them not get over it?
<v Ira Glass>他们中大多数人能克服吗？还是大多数人不能克服？

00:19:35.100 --> 00:19:43.460
<v Rick>I would say it's about 50%.
<v Rick>我想大概是50%。

00:19:43.460 --> 00:20:01.170
<v Ira Glass>Some of things the study says that athletes might focus on if they want to avoid thinking, they can focus on the catcher's mitt or on wherever it is that the ball is supposed to go. Golfers can focus on one word, like a general thought that describes the swing they want, like "smooth."
<v Ira Glass>研究称，如果运动员想避免思考，可以专注于捕手的手套或者球应该去的任何地方。高尔夫球手可以专注于一个词，比如描述他们想要的挥杆的一般想法，比如“平滑”。

00:20:01.170 --> 00:20:27.530
<v Ira Glass>Steve Blass tried everything to get out of his abyss. He tried pitching from his knees. He tried pitching from second base. He tried pitching every night in the bullpen, and he tried to take a week off to see if the rest helped him. He watched himself on video and compared it to video of when he was pitching well. He went to a hypnotist and to a psychologist. He learned meditation and practiced it. When a friend suggested changing to looser underwear, he laughed about it with his teammates. And then, he tried it.
<v Ira Glass>史蒂夫·布拉斯为了走出深渊尝试了一切。他尝试过跪着投球。他尝试过从二垒位置投球。他尝试过每天晚上在牛棚里投球，他尝试过休息一周看看休息是否有帮助。他看自己的录像，并与自己投得好时候的录像进行对比。他去看过催眠师和心理学家。他学习冥想并练习它。当朋友建议换穿更宽松的内衣时，他和队友们一起笑了笑。然后，他试了。

00:20:27.530 --> 00:20:35.230
<v Ira Glass>Some of these cures focused him more on his mechanics. Some of them tried to take his mind off of his mechanics. None of them worked.
<v Ira Glass>其中一些疗法让他更关注自己的力学动作。其中一些试图转移他对力学动作的注意力。没有一个起作用。

