00:37:57.850 --> 00:38:09.390
<v Ben>Yeah, me neither.
<v Ben>是的，我也不在。

00:38:09.390 --> 00:38:38.290
<v Ira Glass>Those actors, Ed Herbstman and Tami Sagher, with Libby George as the mom and Christian Paluck very briefly as the bartender. This story was produced and directed by Jonathan Mitchell, who also did the music. The story was written by Jonathan Mitchell, Ed Herbstman, and Melanie Hoops. If you liked what you just heard, they're doing one like this every two weeks on their podcast that's called The Truth . Their website, thetruthapm.com, or look for The Truth podcast at the iTunes store.
<v Ira Glass>那些演员，埃德·赫布斯特曼（Ed Herbstman）和塔米·萨格（Tami Sagher），还有扮演妈妈的利比·乔治（Libby George）和短暂客串酒保的克里斯蒂安·帕卢克（Christian Paluck）。这个故事由乔纳森·米切尔制作和导演，他也制作了音乐。这个故事由乔纳森·米切尔、埃德·赫布斯特曼和梅兰妮·胡普斯（Melanie Hoops）编写。如果你喜欢刚才听到的，他们每两周会在他们的播客《真相》（The Truth）上制作一个这样的节目。他们的网站是thetruthapm.com，或者在iTunes商店里搜索《真相》播客。

00:38:38.290 --> 00:38:53.840
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, a man who does not approve of homosexuality whose biggest problem is he is homosexual. Stories of being your own worst enemy continue in a minute from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，一个不赞成同性恋却最大的问题是自己也是同性恋的人。关于成为自身最大敌人的故事将在芝加哥公共广播电台和国际公共广播电台节目继续的一分钟后继续播出。

00:38:53.840 --> 00:39:06.870
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme, bring you different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's program, Own Worst Enemy, stories of people who thwart themselves in ways that they do not seem to be able to control.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。每周在我们的节目中，我们都会选择一个主题，为你带来关于这个主题的不同类型的故事。今天的节目主题是“自身最大敌人”，讲述人们以自己似乎无法控制的方式挫败自己的故事。

00:39:06.870 --> 00:39:37.810
<v Ira Glass>We have arrived at Act Three of our show. Act Three, Just As I Am. This last story is about somebody who is at war with himself. He's a gay man, a Christian, who did not want to be gay or act on his feelings for men. And he didn't just want to suppress this impulse in himself. He wasn't just his own worst enemy. He tried to get other gay men and women to suppress that part of themselves as well. But then, through a series of actions that can only be described as very Christian, this man changed. Jonathan Menjivar tells what happened.
<v Ira Glass>我们已经到了节目的第三幕。第三幕，就如我所是（Just As I Am）。最后一个故事是关于一个与自己处于战争状态的人。他是一个同性恋，一个基督徒，他不希望自己是同性恋，也不想顺从对男人的感觉。他不仅仅是想压抑自己内心的这种冲动。他不只是自己的最大敌人。他还试图让其他同性恋男女也压抑自己那部分。但后来，通过一系列只能被描述为非常基督徒的行为，这个人改变了。乔纳森·门吉瓦（Jonathan Menjivar）讲述了发生的事情。

00:39:37.810 --> 00:40:13.390
<v Jonathan>The guy's name was John Smid, and he ran an organization called Love in Action, one of those Christian places that claims they can cure people of their homosexuality. Make them not exactly straight, but ex-gay. It was in Memphis, Tennessee. And at the time, it was the oldest and most prominent ex-gay ministry in the world. One former client told me it was considered the Cadillac of ex-gay programs. The rules were harsh, the level of shame, intense. Former clients have spent years in therapy unwinding what happened to them at Love in Action. And John Smid was the head of that group for more than two decades.
<v Jonathan>那个家伙叫约翰·斯米德（John Smid），他经营着一个叫“生活中的爱”（Love in Action）的组织，这是那种声称能治愈同性恋的基督教机构之一。不是让他们变得完全异性恋，而是“前同性恋”。它位于田纳西州的孟菲斯。在当时，它是世界上最古老、最著名的前同性恋事工。一位前客户告诉我，它被认为是前同性恋项目的“凯迪拉克”。规则很严苛，羞耻感程度很高。前客户们花了多年时间在心理治疗中化解他们在“生活中的爱”经历的一切。约翰·斯米德担任那个小组的负责人超过二十年。

00:40:13.390 --> 00:40:42.340
<v Jonathan>As for his own homosexuality, John never tried to hide it. It was part of his testimony he'd give at church and ex-gay events. John was married at 19. And then, after having two kids, he realized he was gay. He got divorced, had a couple gay relationships. John says he was also pretty promiscuous. And then, in his late 20s, he found god and stopped living as a gay man. This is John giving that testimony on an old Christian TV segment. John says he was in this little, country church in Iowa.
<v Jonathan>至于他自己的同性恋，约翰从没试图隐瞒。这是他在教堂和前同性恋活动中提供的见证的一部分。约翰19岁时结婚。然后，在有了两个孩子后，他意识到自己是同性恋。他离婚了，有过几次同性恋关系。约翰说他也相当滥交。然后，在他快30岁时，他找到了上帝，停止了作为同性恋的生活。这是约翰在一段老基督教电视节目中提供的见证。约翰说他在爱荷华州的一个小乡村教堂里。

00:40:42.340 --> 00:41:20.740
<v John>I just remember sitting in the pew. And this voice spoke into my brain, "John," called my name, "you don't have to live like this anymore." It was just that point when God spoke, and I gained permission to ask for freedom. And I started praying every day. I just kept saying every day, God, get me out of this. Get me out of this. I'm miserable. God, get me out of this. And eventually, I got to the point where I left the partners and the relationships. And that was the end of my homosexuality.
<v John>我只记得坐在长椅上。一个声音在我的脑海中响起，“约翰，”叫着我的名字，“你不必再这样生活了。”就在上帝说话的那一刻，我获得了寻求自由的许可。我开始每天祈祷。我只是每天不停地说：上帝，让我脱离这个。让我脱离这个。我很痛苦。上帝，让我脱离这个。最终，我达到了那种离开伙伴和关系的程度。这就是我同性恋生活的终结。

00:41:20.740 --> 00:41:36.730
<v Jonathan>John made a new life for himself, dated women, and then he married one, his second wife. he soon got a job encouraging other gay men to live the way he was. And then, almost 20 years after John joined Love in Action, he decided to expand their mission.
<v Jonathan>约翰为自己开启了新生活，和女性约会，然后嫁给了一个女人，他的第二任妻子。他很快找到了一份鼓励其他同性恋者过他那种生活的工作。然后，在约翰加入“生活中的爱”近20年后，他决定扩大他们的使命。

00:41:36.730 --> 00:41:59.340
<v Jonathan>Love in Action had always been for adults. But John says he was seeing a dramatic increase in teens coming. Their parents were calling Love in Action more and more. So they started a new program specifically for teenagers. At the time, Love in Action had just bought a new building. It was step up for them. Then, John came to work. It was the first day of the new teen program.
<v Jonathan>“生活中的爱”一直都是针对成人的。但约翰说他看到青少年来访的数量激增。他们的父母越来越多地打电话给“生活中的爱”。所以他们开始了一个专门针对青少年的新项目。当时，“生活中的爱”刚刚买了一栋新大楼。这对他们来说是向前迈出了一步。然后，约翰去上班。那是青少年新项目的第一天。

00:41:59.340 --> 00:42:11.540
<v John>It was 8:30, June 6, 2005, sitting in my office. And one of my staff members came in and said, a client just came and told us that there's a big protest outside.
<v John>那是2005年6月6日早上8:30，坐在我的办公室里。我的一个员工走进来，说一位客户刚来告诉我们外面有个大规模抗议活动。

00:42:11.540 --> 00:42:12.630
<v Protesters>It's OK to be gay!
<v Protesters>同性恋没问题！

00:42:12.630 --> 00:42:13.960
<v Jonathan>And then, what did you do?
<v Jonathan>然后，你做了什么？

00:42:13.960 --> 00:42:14.910
<v John>Freaked out.
<v John>吓坏了。

00:42:14.910 --> 00:42:17.860
<v Protesters>It's OK to be gay!
<v Protesters>同性恋没问题！

00:42:17.860 --> 00:42:29.120
<v John>I didn't know what to do. I felt incredibly exposed. It was the first day in our building. We had just moved in.
<v John>我不知道该怎么办。我感到极其脆弱。那是我们搬进这栋大楼的第一天。我们刚搬进来。

00:42:29.120 --> 00:42:34.600
<v Girl>Homosexuality is not a sin. Homosexuality is not an addiction!
<v Girl>同性恋不是罪。同性恋不是成瘾！

00:42:34.600 --> 00:43:04.350
<v Jonathan>The new program for teens was called Refuge, and it's why the protesters were there. Love in Action was a voluntary program. But Refuge was different, at least according to a 16-year-old kid named Zach Stark. A week earlier, Zach had posted on his MySpace page saying that he had come out and his parents didn't take it well. So they were forcing him to go to Refuge. "They tell me that there is something psychologically wrong with me," Zach wrote. "I'm a big screw up to them who isn't on the path God wants me to be on."
<v Jonathan>这个针对青少年的新项目叫“避难所”（Refuge），这就是抗议者在那里的原因。“生活中的爱”是一个自愿项目。但“避难所”不同，至少根据一个叫扎克·斯塔克（Zach Stark）的16岁孩子所说。一周前，扎克在MySpace页面上发帖称他已经出柜，但他父母对此反应不佳。所以他们强迫他去“避难所”。“他们告诉我我在心理上有什么问题，”扎克写道。“在他们眼中我是一个很大的失败者，没有走在上帝希望我走的道路上。”

00:43:04.350 --> 00:43:07.470
<v Morgan>It was impossible not to want to help him.
<v Morgan>不去想帮助他是不可能的。

00:43:07.470 --> 00:43:29.260
<v Jonathan>This is Morgan Jon Fox. He's a Memphis filmmaker, and he was pretty much the ringleader of the protests. He'd read Zach's blog and pulled together other activists and a bunch of Zach's friends from high school. Morgan is gay himself, and he filmed a lot of the protest. He and all the other protesters couldn't believe what they were reading on Zach's blog. To them, it seemed like a cry for help.
<v Jonathan>这是摩根·琼·福克斯（Morgan Jon Fox）。他是一位孟菲斯电影制作人，基本上是抗议活动的领袖。他读了扎克的博客，把其他活动家和扎克的一群高中朋友召集在一起。摩根自己也是同性恋，他拍摄了大量抗议活动。他和所有其他抗议者都不敢相信他们在扎克的博客上读到的东西。对他们来说，这看起来像是一个求救信号。

00:43:29.260 --> 00:43:43.320
<v Morgan>And then, he goes on to explain that he ran away for a day, and now he's come back. And he's writing these blog entries in the middle of the night. And that if he gets caught, you know, it could be very bad. And then, he went on to post the rules of this organization.
<v Morgan>然后，他继续解释说他跑掉了一天，现在又回来了。他在深夜写这些博客条目。如果被抓住，你知道，这可能会非常糟糕。然后，他继续发布了这个组织的规则。

00:43:43.320 --> 00:44:14.970
<v Jonathan>The kids in Love in Action's teen program went home to their parents every night. But they had to adhere to the rules no matter where they were. There was no hugging or physical contact allowed, though brief handshakes or an affirmative hand on a shoulder were OK. Any, quote, "temptations, fantasies, or dreams" were supposed to be confessed to a staff member. Men were supposed to dress like men, and women like women. And for some reason, no one was allowed to wear anything by Abercrombie & Fitch or Calvin Klein.
<v Jonathan>“生活中的爱”青少年项目的孩子们每天回家见父母。但无论他们在哪里，都必须遵守规则。不允许任何拥抱或身体接触，尽管短暂握手或在肩膀上给予肯定的拍打是可以的。任何所谓的“诱惑、幻想或梦想”都应该向工作人员坦白。男人应该像男人一样着装，女人应该像女人一样。而且由于某种原因，不允许任何人穿Abercrombie & Fitch或Calvin Klein的任何东西。

00:44:14.970 --> 00:44:27.410
<v Jonathan>The protests clearly had two intended targets-- Zach, who they were there to support, and John Smid, the guy they were shouting at through their bull horns. Morgan was there for weeks, making every attempt to get through to John.
<v Jonathan>抗议活动显然有两个预定的目标——扎克（他们去支持的人），和约翰·斯米德（他们通过扩音器叫喊的人）。摩根在那里待了几个星期，竭尽全力想接触约翰。

00:44:27.410 --> 00:44:33.110
<v Morgan>We knew that John Smid was the head of the organization. And essentially, he was our enemy in this situation.
<v Morgan>我们知道约翰·斯米德是该组织的负责人。从本质上讲，他是我们这种情况下的敌人。

00:44:33.110 --> 00:44:36.040
<v Girl>Why they can't question your policies!
<v Girl>为什么他们不能质疑你们的政策！

00:44:36.040 --> 00:44:38.470
<v Protesters>Why, John Smid?
<v Protesters>为什么，约翰·斯米德？

00:44:38.470 --> 00:45:05.810
<v Morgan>He never came and addressed us. There was one day, he was driving. And he stopped for one moment. And we made sure at that very moment we saw him, because it was kind of like this-- man, I don't know. It's like seeing Sasquatch. You know, we never got to see him. And then, so when we would actually see him in person, it was like, OK, this is our moment. We gotta do something right, or we've got to prove something.
<v Morgan>他从未出来对我们发表讲话。有一天，他正在开车。他停了一瞬间。我们在那一刻确保看到了他，因为那就像……天哪，我不知道。就像看到大脚怪一样。你知道，我们从来没能见到他。所以当我们真正亲眼见到他时，就像是：好的，这是我们的时刻。我们要把事情做对，或者我们必须证明点什么。

00:45:05.810 --> 00:45:24.520
<v Jonathan>The protests at Love in Action became a national news story. They got picked up by CNN, the New York Times , and Good Morning America . Eight weeks later when it was all over and Zach Stark had finished the program, Morgan wanted to interview John Smid for the documentary he'd started working on about the protests. John says he didn't want anything to do with Morgan.
<v Jonathan>“生活中的爱”的抗议活动成为了全国新闻事件。它们被CNN、《纽约时报》和《早安美国》报道。八周后，当一切结束，扎克·斯塔克完成了项目，摩根想采访约翰·斯米德，为了他开始制作的关于抗议活动的纪录片。约翰说他不想和摩根有任何关系。

00:45:24.520 --> 00:45:56.940
<v John>Of course, I understood he had this organization called Queer Action Coalition. And it was just this horribly negative thing that I just don't want anything to do with. and he called and wanted to meet with me. And I said, OK, how do I set up the safeguards? Because you've got to be careful when these gay activists come around. You know, they're going to twist your words. And somehow, they've got an alternative agenda. And they just want to use your words negatively. So I asked one of my staff members to come in and meet with me with him because I didn't want to be him alone because I thought, certainly I need some kind of accountability here.
<v John>当然，我知道他有一个叫“酷儿行动联盟”（Queer Action Coalition）的组织。那简直是一个极其负面的东西，我根本不想和它有任何牵扯。他打电话来想要见我。我说：好的，我该如何设置保障措施？因为你必须小心那些同性恋激进分子出现在周围。你知道，他们会歪曲你的话。不知何故，他们有一个替代议程。他们只想消极地利用你的话。所以我请我的一名员工和我一起见他，因为我不想单独见他，因为我想：我肯定需要某种问责制。

00:45:56.940 --> 00:46:28.000
<v Morgan>And so I walk into his office. The meeting is at Love in Action headquarters. I walk in. And he sits down behind this big desk. And one of his staff members sits down beside him. And I sit across from this big table. And the night before, I had really thought about things I would say, and having this opportunity, and possibly I would debate him. You know, I wanted to have an eloquent argument. I had like a six page document that I had prepared and studied, hoping that those were points that I would hit.
<v Morgan>于是我走进他的办公室。会议在“生活中的爱”总部举行。我走进去。他坐在那张大办公桌后面。他的一名员工坐在他旁边。我坐在那张大桌子对面。前一天晚上，我真的思考过我要说的话，有这个机会，而且我也可能和他辩论。你知道，我想要一个雄辩的论点。我有一份我准备和研究了六页的文件，希望能成为我击中的点。

00:46:28.000 --> 00:46:46.630
<v Morgan>But I sit across the table from him. And he's like, well? And I'm like, yeah? And then, literally like 10 seconds of silence. I'm sitting there. No one is saying anything. And I'm just like, oh my god. And the last thing that was crossing my mind at that moment was to debate or start arguing with him. And so I just started telling him my story.
<v Morgan>但我坐在他对面。他像是：那么？我像是：是啊？然后，真的有大概10秒钟的沉默。我坐在那里。没人说话。我心想：噢，天哪。那一刻在我脑海中闪过的最后一件事就是辩论或开始和他争吵。所以我只是开始告诉他我的故事。

00:46:46.630 --> 00:47:20.400
<v Morgan>I just started telling him about my childhood, when I came out, all the things that I faced, the pain that I felt of not feeling necessarily accepted by peers or family, and about that process of going through all of that, but then being in a relationship, and being out, and feeling loved, and feeling a part of a community here in Memphis, and how much healthier I felt. And then, went on to explain, that's why I protested your organization all of these past few weeks.
<v Morgan>我只是开始告诉他我的童年，我出柜的时候，我面对的所有事情，我不被同龄人或家庭接受的痛苦，以及经历这一切的过程，然后是在一段关系中，出柜，感到被爱，感到在孟菲斯这里是社区的一部分，以及我感到多么健康。然后，继续解释这就是为什么我在过去几周抗议你们组织的原因。

00:47:20.400 --> 00:47:50.020
<v John>And I thought, wait a minute. This is not what I expected. This guy seems to be vulnerable, and honest, and humble. And it's like, this isn't-- I don't hate this guy. How could I hate him? He just came in my office, and treated me with respect, and was honest himself. And he seemed open, and I didn't know what to do with it.
<v John>我心想：等等。这不是我预期的。这家伙看起来很脆弱，诚实，谦逊。这就像……我不恨这家伙。我怎么可能恨他？他刚才走进我的办公室，用尊重对待我，自己也很诚实。他看起来很开放，我不知道该怎么办。

00:47:50.020 --> 00:48:12.040
<v Jonathan>They talked for half an hour. And then, Morgan asked John if he'd agree to an interview. John turned him down. For a year after that meeting, there was a kind of ceasefire. John and Morgan didn't talk to each other at all. Then, Morgan and some of the other protesters attended an open meeting at Love in Action. Morgan says they were just trying to keep an eye on them, see what Love in Action were up to.
<v Jonathan>他们交谈了半小时。然后，摩根问约翰是否同意接受采访。约翰拒绝了他。那次会议后有一年时间，有一种停火。约翰和摩根根本没有交谈。然后，摩根和其他一些抗议者参加了“生活中的爱”的一次公开会议。摩根说他们只是想盯着他们，看看“生活中的爱”在搞什么。

00:48:12.040 --> 00:48:48.950
<v Morgan>And it was funny because the thing that John talked about at that open meeting was this idea of how we respond to our parents, or how we kind of communicate with them, and deal with the wounds that might be there. It was interesting because at the time, I wasn't that close with my father. And surprisingly, I'm sitting there listening to him say this stuff, and something just clicks in my head that I had been kind of attempting to relate to my father for a long time on my terms only, and not looking at him as a person with the full emotions and issues that he maybe is dealing with.
<v Morgan>很有趣，因为约翰在那次公开会议上谈论的事情是关于我们如何回应父母，或者我们如何与他们沟通，并处理可能存在的伤口。这很有趣，因为当时我和我父亲不太亲近。出人意料的是，我坐在那里听他说这些东西，我脑子里突然闪过一个念头：我一直试图只按我的方式与父亲联系，而不是把他看作一个有着完整情感和问题、可能正在处理的人。

00:48:48.950 --> 00:49:16.170
<v Morgan>And so that was the building blocks towards me becoming closer with my father and mending that relationship. And so that's something that I was extremely grateful for. And so I followed up that meeting in sending John an email, and was like, hey, look, I still totally disagree with everything you're doing. However, I just want to let you know that it's pretty cool that I learned something from your meeting. So just wanted to say thanks, and I appreciate that part of it, even though the rest of it needs to go.
<v Morgan>所以那是通向我与父亲变得更亲近、修复那段关系的积木。所以我对此感到非常感激。所以我在那次会议后给约翰发了一封电子邮件，像是：嘿，看，我仍然完全不同意你正在做的一切。然而，我只是想让你知道，我从你的会议中学到点东西真是太酷了。所以只是想说谢谢，我很欣赏那部分，尽管剩下的部分需要滚蛋。

00:49:16.170 --> 00:49:43.840
<v John>And so I'm thinking, OK, this doesn't match again. My favorite enemy protester is actually complementing me. And he's actually saying that I've had a positive impact. And that kind of blew me away. And so I emailed him back. And I said, Morgan, you know, I would really like to talk with you personally. We should go out and have coffee if you'd like to do that because I've got some things I want to share with you.
<v John>于是我心想：好的，这又不匹配了。我最喜欢的敌对抗议者实际上在恭维我。而且他实际上在说我产生了积极的影响。这让我大吃一惊。所以我回信说：摩根，你知道，我真的很想和你个人谈谈。如果你愿意，我们应该出去喝杯咖啡，因为我有一些事情想和你分享。

00:49:43.840 --> 00:49:49.330
<v Jonathan>Morgan and John agreed to meet at a Starbucks. Morgan says the coffee lasted three hours.
<v Jonathan>摩根和约翰同意在星巴克见面。摩根说咖啡喝了三个小时。

00:49:49.330 --> 00:49:52.830
<v Jonathan>In these three hours, what are you guys talking about?
<v Jonathan>在这三个小时里，你们在聊什么？

00:49:52.830 --> 00:50:13.440
<v Morgan>I mean, literally we're talking about what's been going on. He's asking me questions about, oh, so you're a filmmaker? I looked you up on the internet a little bit. You know, that kind of stuff. And so I'm talking about my films. Basically, we talk about anything but the issues, which is fascinating. I mean, we're literally talking about my job at this coffee shop.
<v Morgan>我是说，我们真的是在聊一直在发生的事情。他问我关于：噢，所以你是电影制作人？我在互联网上稍微查了你一下。你知道，那种东西。所以我谈论我的电影。基本上，我们谈论除了问题之外的任何事情，这很有趣。我是说，我们真的是在聊我在咖啡店的工作。

00:50:13.440 --> 00:50:17.050
<v Jonathan>Yeah, I was going to say it's almost like an awkward first date.
<v Jonathan>是啊，我正想说，这几乎就像一次尴尬的第一次约会。

00:50:17.050 --> 00:50:23.590
<v Morgan>[LAUGHS] That's awkward.
<v Morgan>[笑声] 那很尴尬。

00:50:23.590 --> 00:50:32.770
<v Jonathan>And not that there's something romantic going on. But are you concerned at any point to that he actually has a crush on you?
<v Jonathan>并不是说发生了什么浪漫的事。但你在任何时候担心过他真的暗恋你吗？

00:50:32.770 --> 00:50:46.840
<v Morgan>I was never concerned about him having a crush on me. I can't say a lot of my friends or my significant other wasn't concerned about that. I mean, certainly that was a joke by a lot of people. Like, oh, he's just trying to hook up with you.
<v Morgan>我从没担心过他会暗恋我。我不能说我的很多朋友或我的伴侣对此不担心。我是说，当然那是很多人的一个笑话。像是：噢，他只是在试图和你搞上。

00:50:46.840 --> 00:51:19.290
<v John>Oh, yeah. I had a couple of friends ask me, are you having an affair with Morgan? People were concerned about us meeting. You know, you shouldn't meet with Morgan alone because you could be tempted. I don't meet with single women or with married women by myself, so you shouldn't meet with a gay man by yourself. You know, is that going to be a problem for you? And I'm thinking, no. Sorry, Morgan, I have not had any romantic inclinations towards Morgan. [LAUGHTER]
<v John>噢，是啊。我有几个朋友问我：你在和摩根有外遇吗？人们很担心我们见面。你知道，你不应该独自见摩根，因为你可能会受到诱惑。我从不单独见单身女性或已婚女性，所以你不应该独自见一个同性恋男人。你知道，这会对你有问题吗？我心想：不。抱歉，摩根，我没有对摩根有过任何浪漫倾向。[笑声]

00:51:19.290 --> 00:51:50.350
<v Jonathan>The Fox-Smid Summits continued. But the Refuge program for teens didn't. John shut it down. And over coffee, he told Morgan that Refuge was a bad idea. Morgan says it was the first time he'd heard John admit that he had done anything wrong at Love in Action. It wasn't exactly a change of heart, though. John still believed he could provide ex-gay services to teens. Refuge, he thought, just turned out to be the wrong way to do it. But as time went on, Morgan felt that something significant was starting to shift in John.
<v Jonathan>福克斯-斯米德峰会继续进行。但针对青少年的“避难所”项目没有。约翰关闭了它。在喝咖啡时，他告诉摩根，“避难所”是个坏主意。摩根说这是他第一次听到约翰承认他在“生活中的爱”做过任何错误的事。但这并不完全是改过自新。约翰仍然相信他可以为青少年提供“前同性恋”服务。“避难所”，他认为，只是被证明是错误的方法。但随着时间流逝，摩根感觉到约翰身上开始有一些重要的东西在转变。

00:51:50.350 --> 00:52:02.560
<v Morgan>He would ask questions to me. I mean, he was being more willing to hear feedback, and would specifically ask, well, what did you think about this? Or what was it about this? Or he would sort of--
<v Morgan>他会问我问题。我是说，他更愿意听取反馈，并会具体问：好吧，你对此怎么想？或者关于这个是什么？或者他会某种程度上……

00:52:02.560 --> 00:52:07.540
<v Jonathan>Like using you as a focus group to see how Love in Action was working?
<v Jonathan>就像把你当作焦点小组来看看“生活中的爱”是如何运作的？

00:52:07.540 --> 00:52:24.770
<v Morgan>Yeah, yeah. I mean, I think he even joked at some point about-- he had said something on the news about, "Well, you know, it's just like having to eat your peas. Sometimes, you have to do things you don't want to do," in response to these kids needing to go into Refuge. And I was like, John, are you kidding me? Like, having to eat your peas? And he laughed and was like, what? That didn't work?
<v Morgan>是啊，是啊。我是说，我想他甚至在某一点开过玩笑说……他在新闻上说过一些东西：嗯，你知道，这就像不得不吃你的豌豆。有时，你不得不做你不想做的事情，以回应这些孩子需要进入“避难所”。我像是：约翰，你在开玩笑吗？像是不得不吃你的豌豆？他笑了笑说：什么？那不起作用？

00:52:24.770 --> 00:52:47.650
<v Morgan>I mean, tell me, seriously, if that didn't work. I'm like, [SCOFFS]. But it's just like, are you serious? So definitely, things became-- though we weren't debating the issues, it became certainly more comfortable for me. I felt more comfortable to straight up say, like, come on, are you serious about this? Can you actually believe this? And he was listening.
<v Morgan>我是说，严肃地告诉我，如果那不起作用的话。我像是[嗤之以鼻]。但这就像，你是认真的吗？所以事情绝对变得……尽管我们没有辩论这些问题，但对我来说肯定变得更舒服了。我感到更舒服地直说，像是：拜托，你是认真的吗？你真的能相信这个？他在听。

00:52:47.650 --> 00:53:18.910
<v Jonathan>What happened between John and Morgan is the thing that almost never happens between people on opposite sides of a political issue. They sat down, and talked, and really listened to each other. Essentially, Morgan did a very Christian thing. At that first meeting, he turned the other cheek. John saw that, and did the same. And it had an effect. John says that Morgan was the first gay person he met who wasn't in conflict over his sexuality. Morgan seemed normal and healthy. And it surprised John.
<v Jonathan>约翰和摩根之间发生的事情是几乎从未在政治问题对立面的人之间发生过的事情。他们坐下来，交谈，并真正倾听彼此。从本质上讲，摩根做了一件非常基督徒的事情。在第一次会议上，他转过了另一半脸。约翰看到了，并做了同样的事。这产生了效果。约翰说摩根是他遇到的第一个不因性取向而感到冲突的同性恋者。摩根看起来很正常和健康。这让约翰感到惊讶。

00:53:18.910 --> 00:53:44.500
<v Jonathan>In March, 2008, John quietly resigned from Love in Action. It would be really satisfying if I could tell you that all that listening, all the sitting down over coffee with Morgan snapped John out of the ex-gay stupor he'd been in for more than two decades. But that's not true. John told me when he left Love in Action, he hadn't actually changed his belief at all. He was still ex-gay, still believed that being gay was a sin.
<v Jonathan>2008年3月，约翰安静地辞去了“生活中的爱”的职务。如果我能告诉你所有这些倾听、所有与摩根一起喝咖啡让他脱离了他陷入二十多年的“前同性恋”昏迷状态，那会非常令人满足。但这不属实。约翰告诉我他离开“生活中的爱”时，他的信念其实一点都没变。他仍然是“前同性恋”，仍然相信同性恋是罪。

00:53:44.500 --> 00:54:18.240
<v Jonathan>Then, last year, John posted this stunning admission on the website of a new ministry he'd started. He said that he'd seen dramatic changes happen in people's lives when they walk through the transformation process with Jesus. But he wrote, quote, "The transformation for the vast majority of homosexuals will not include a change of sexual orientation. Actually, I've never met a man who experienced a change from homosexual to heterosexual." That statement coming from John made news. Here's John with Chris Matthews on Hardball last fall.
<v Jonathan>然后，去年，约翰在他开始的一个新事工网站上发布了这条惊人的告白。他说他看到当人们与耶稣一起走过转化过程时，人们的生活发生了巨大的变化。但他写道，引用：“绝大多数同性恋者的转化将不包括性取向的改变。事实上，我从未见过一个经历从同性恋转变为异性恋的人。”那句来自约翰的话上了新闻。这是去年秋天约翰在《硬球》（Hardball）节目上与克里斯·马修斯（Chris Matthews）的对话。

00:54:18.240 --> 00:54:35.740
<v Chris Matthews>John, I guess the question a lot of people, gay and straight, will ask you is what brought you to go public with this pretty important observation since you've been experienced in this world that no one's ever gone through a successful, if you will, or conversion of any kind from gay to straight?
<v Chris Matthews>约翰，我想很多人，同性恋和异性恋，会问你的是什么让你公开了这个相当重要的观察结果，因为你在那个世界里有经验，没有人经历过成功的，如果你愿意，或者是从同性恋到异性恋的任何形式的转变？

00:54:35.740 --> 00:54:40.770
<v John>I think really one of the most significant things, Chris, is that within the gay community, there is--
<v John>我想实际上最重要的事情之一是，克里斯，在同性恋社区内，有……

00:54:40.770 --> 00:55:11.650
<v Jonathan>I want to make sure this is clear. John now says God embraces homosexuality, that God loves gays and lesbians, and He sees nothing wrong with their actions. He now fully supports faithful, same-sex commitments. And he started a ministry that reaches out to gay people. But the new John Smid isn't exactly a crusader for the gay movement. He's still learning to navigate who  he is. And whatever transformation he's going through, it's still incomplete. He's working it out in public. And it's clumsy.
<v Jonathan>我想确保这一点很清楚。约翰现在说上帝拥抱同性恋，上帝爱同性恋者，他认为他们的行为没问题。他现在完全支持忠诚的同性承诺。他开始了一个接触同性恋者的事工。但现在的约翰·斯米德并不完全是同性恋运动的十字军战士。他仍在学习导航他是谁。无论他正在经历什么转变，它仍然是不完整的。他正在公众场合解决这个问题。而且很笨拙。

00:55:11.650 --> 00:55:47.750
<v Jonathan>There've been times on his blog when he's used the word "perversion" while talking about homosexuality. He talks about promiscuity like it's something that gay men just can't avoid without help from God. The things he says now can still be so hurtful and confusing that a former client told me he wishes John would just lay low for a few years until he figures out what he wants to say. Morgan eventually got the interview he was looking for. He and John sat down and talked on camera. And now, they're doing Q&As together, talking to audiences about the documentary Morgan made.
<v Jonathan>在他的博客上曾经有几次，他在谈论同性恋时使用了“反常”这个词。他谈论滥交就像这是同性恋男人在没有上帝帮助下无法避免的事情一样。他现在说的话仍然可能非常伤人和令人困惑，以至于一位前客户告诉我，他希望约翰能沉寂几年，直到他弄清楚他想说什么。摩根最终得到了他想要的采访。他和约翰坐下来在镜头前交谈。现在，他们一起做问答，和观众谈论摩根制作的纪录片。

00:55:47.750 --> 00:56:18.570
<v Jonathan>Recently, John made probably his biggest shift. He started calling himself gay. Not ex-gay, not ex-ex-gay, just gay. John and his wife are still married. It's obviously confusing for the two of them. But this is the uncomfortable place John Smid has lived in his whole life. When he was in his first marriage in his late teens, he struggled because he was gay. Then, he came out and he struggled with that, so he became ex-gay.
<v Jonathan>最近，约翰做了他可能最大的转变。他开始称自己为“同性恋”。不是“前同性恋”，不是“前前同性恋”，只是同性恋。约翰和他的妻子仍然结婚。这对他们俩来说显然很困惑。但这就是约翰·斯米德一生中居住的那个不舒服的地方。当他在青少年后期第一次婚姻中时，他挣扎是因为他是同性恋。然后，他出柜了，他对此挣扎，所以他成了“前同性恋”。

00:56:18.570 --> 00:56:47.170
<v Jonathan>We'd all like to believe that, as we get older, we know ourselves better and things get easier. But it doesn't always work out that way. John says now, he's kind of back to where he was in his early 20s-- gay and out, just a lot older this time. He's still really conflicted about what that means.
<v Jonathan>我们都愿意相信，随着我们年龄增长，我们更了解自己，事情会变得更容易。但情况并不总是那样。约翰说现在，他有点回到他20出头时的状态了——同性恋并出柜，只是这次老多了。他仍然对他意味着什么感到非常矛盾。

00:56:47.170 --> 00:57:20.240
<v Ira Glass>Jonathan Menjivar is one of the producers of our program. More information about the documentary that Morgan Jon Fox made is at loveinactionmovie.com.
<v Ira Glass>乔纳森·门吉瓦是我们节目的制作人之一。关于摩根·琼·福克斯制作的纪录片的更多信息在loveinactionmovie.com。

00:57:20.240 --> 00:57:30.280
<v Ira Glass>[ACKNOWLEDGMENTS]
<v Ira Glass>[致谢]

00:57:30.280 --> 00:57:53.240
<v Ira Glass>Thanks to Steve Blass's co-author, Erik Sherman. Their book goes on sale May 1. Thanks for help with our radio drama to Peter Clowning, Carrie Helmen, Chris Bannon, and WNYC. Rick Wolff, who you heard in Act One, does a radio show about sports parenting on New York's WFAN. He can be found on askcoachwolff.com. Major League Baseball footage today was used with permission of Major League Baseball Properties, Inc.
<v Ira Glass>感谢史蒂夫·布拉斯的共同作者埃里克·谢尔曼（Erik Sherman）。他们的书将于5月1日开售。感谢彼得·克劳宁（Peter Clowning）、凯莉·海尔曼（Carrie Helmen）、克里斯·班农（Chris Bannon）和WNYC对我们广播剧的帮助。你在第一幕听到的里克·沃尔夫在纽约的WFAN做关于运动教养的广播节目。他可以在askcoachwolff.com找到。今天的美国职业棒球大联盟录像是在美国职业棒球大联盟资产公司的许可下使用的。

00:57:53.240 --> 00:58:23.910
<v Ira Glass>Our website, where right now you can enter our contest to win a signed poster for our May 10 cinema event-- basically, we're going to be doing our show on stage with Mike Birbiglia, David Rakoff, Glenn Washington, and others and beaming it into movie theaters everywhere. Find a theater near you and get tickets before they sell out. You can do all that at thisamericanlife.org. This American Life is distributed by Public Radio International. WBEZ management oversight for our program by our boss, Mr. Torey Malatia, who has totally figured out what we're up to here at the end of the program when we quote him.
<v Ira Glass>我们的网站，现在你可以进入我们的竞赛，赢得我们5月10日电影活动的签名海报——基本上，我们将和迈克·比比利亚（Mike Birbiglia）、大卫·拉考夫（David Rakoff）、格伦·华盛顿（Glenn Washington）等人一起在舞台上表演我们的节目，并将其照射到各地的电影院。在你附近的电影院找到它，在它们售罄之前买票。你可以做所有这些在thisamericanlife.org。《美国生活》由国际公共广播电台发行。WBEZ对我们节目的管理监督由我们的老板托里·马拉蒂亚（Torey Malatia）先生负责，他在节目结束时我们引用他的时候，完全弄清楚了我们在做什么。

00:58:23.910 --> 00:58:30.860
<v Torey>You've got to be careful when these gay activists come around, you know. They're going to twist your words. And somehow, they've got an alternative agenda.
<v Torey>你必须小心那些同性恋激进分子出现在周围，你知道。他们会歪曲你的话。不知何故，他们有一个替代议程。

00:58:30.860 --> 00:58:54.860
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass, back next week with more stories of This American Life .
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯，下周带着更多《美国生活》的故事回来。

00:58:54.860 --> 00:58:59.860
<v Announcer>PRI, Public Radio International.
<v Announcer>PRI，国际公共广播电台。
